  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "sorting gets you O(n log n) - the problem asks for O(n). what data structure gives you O(1) 'does this value exist?' checks?",
    "put every number in a hash set. for a given number n, how would you know whether it's the START of a consecutive run, rather than somewhere in the middle?",
    "a number n is the start of a run only if n - 1 is NOT in the set. only bother counting forward (n+1, n+2, ...) from numbers that pass that check",
    "this guarantees each number is only ever the start of at most one run you count from, keeping the total work across the whole array O(n)"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "starting a count from every number instead of only from run-starts, which degrades to O(n^2) on adversarial input",
    "sorting the array, which works but doesn't meet the O(n) requirement",
    "not deduplicating repeated values before or during the count, causing inflated run lengths"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an unsorted array of integers.\n\n**Find the length of the longest run of consecutive integers that all\nappear somewhere in the array, in O(n) time.**\n\nThe run doesn't need to appear contiguously in the array itself, just\nexist as consecutive values.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= nums.length <= 10^5",
      "-10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "empty array (answer 0)",
      "all the same number repeated",
      "no two numbers are consecutive"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
